	Language revitalization is not merely the documentation of a language, its lexicon, and its grammar, itsusage, etc. Such documentation efforts are often a necessary part of language revitalization, especially when a given language community is no longer socially connected such that there is regular conversational exchange between members in the language belonging to that community. In such cases, the glue that keeps phonology and grammar consistent across families and enclaves tends to erode, and resources like dictionaries and grammars can help provide points of coordination. But, the production of these resources alone must be linked to an uptake on the part of the community in order to do any good with revitalizing a language.